/* SHORE_FISH */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain SNOW

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_56a - does an object name inside a comment open a nested comment, or
close the one it is in?
Feeds: parser-design Sec.2.1 and Sec.13 item 7, the token-alias import,
       and every script in this directory

THIS HEADER IS AT THE BOTTOM OF THE FILE ON PURPOSE. The test is the first line,
and anything above it would change what is being measured.

WHERE THIS CAME FROM
RMSTEST_42 generated a blank grass map on its first run. The cause was not in
its script at all: the shore-fish constant appears in its explanatory header
comment, and removing it - by putting a space in the middle of the word - made
the same file run correctly. So a word inside a comment ended the comment early,
or started a new one, and either way the engine stopped reading the file as its
author wrote it.

That is the aliasing question this project has carried as an open item for
several revisions, and it now has a live specimen. Sec.2.1 says words and
numbers can alias structural tokens because the engine resolves every token
through one symbol table, and the comment markers are ordinary tokens in that
table. What was never established is that a CONSTANT can alias a COMMENT MARKER,
which is worse than aliasing a brace: it changes how much of the file exists.

WHICH MARKER, AND WHY IT MATTERS WHICH
  If it OPENS a comment, comments nest, so the closing marker on the same line
  closes only the inner one and THE REST OF THE FILE STAYS COMMENTED OUT. The
  script is never read. A blank map.

  If it CLOSES a comment, the comment ends at the word, everything after it on
  those lines becomes live script, and the closing marker at the end is a stray
  token. The script still runs, but with a stretch of English prose parsed as
  commands first.

The blank map that RMSTEST_42 produced fits OPENING much better than closing.
Under the closing reading, 42's real sections would still have been reached and
it would have generated its water and grass map with a pile of junk diagnostics,
not a blank one. But that is an inference from one accident and this file
measures it directly.

METHOD
The first line of this file is a comment containing nothing but the word. The
script below it paints the whole map snow, which nothing else could produce by
accident.

  BLANK or grass map -> the word OPENS a comment. The rest of the file was never
                        read. This is the destructive reading.
  SNOW map           -> the word CLOSES the comment. The stray marker was
                        harmless and the script ran.

56b is this file with the word removed from the comment. It must come out snow.
If it does not, the pair says nothing.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. ONE RUN EACH. Binary.

WHAT TO DO WITH THE ANSWER, EITHER WAY
This directory's scripts carry long explanatory headers by design, and that
design has now silently voided one run. The house rule adopted from RMSTEST_44
onward is that no header comment contains a constant in full uppercase. Confirm
or refine that rule here.

Then sweep, because one name is not a set. The engine's own constant file is on
disk and the token-alias import has been blocked on exactly this question - the
useful output is the LIST of constants whose value collides with a comment
marker, not just this one. That list also decides whether the parser should
report the collision, which it currently cannot: it treats comments as a
token-stream construct and resolves no constants inside them, so it sees a
comment where the engine sees the end of the file.
*/
